body:
added toaster for notifications and alerts using sonner library

import:
```
import { toast } from 'sonner';
```

basic usage just call:
```
toast('This is an normal toast');

toast.error('This is an error toast');

toast.success('This is an success toast');

toast('This is an action toast', {
          action: {
            label: 'Action',
            onClick: () => console.log('Action!'),
          },
        });

toast('My cancel toast', {
  cancel: <Button onClick={() => console.log('Cancel!')}>Cancel</Button>,
});
```

more usage check here: https://sonner.emilkowal.ski/toast

Usage example - Conditional Rendering Based on App Mode:
```typescript
import { appMode } from "~/signals";

function ConditionalComponent() {
  return (
    <div className="app-container">
      {/* Show/hide components based on current mode */}
      {appMode.value === 'image' && (
        <div className="image-tools">
          <h3>Image Mode Tools</h3>
        </div>
      )}

      {appMode.value === 'edit' && (
        <div className="edit-tools">
          <h3>Edit Mode Tools</h3>
        </div>
      )}
  );
}
```

Remove macOS release diagnostics that can expose decrypted signing-key material in workflow output.

The certificate import workflow used "openssl pkcs12 ... -nodes" while inspecting the signing archive. Because "-nodes" emits private keys unencrypted, diagnostic output could include signing-key material.

This change:

- removes diagnostics that decrypt or print private-key contents;
- changes the PKCS#12 structure check to explicitly use "-nokeys";
- preserves certificate-only subject, issuer, validity, and type diagnostics;
- leaves the actual certificate import and signing flow unchanged.

Closes #2250.

Prevent integer overflow when converting very large Kinovi credit amounts to estimated USD cents.

"from_kinovi_credits()" previously calculated "kinovi_credits * 100" as a "u64". Values above "u64::MAX / 100" could therefore panic in checked builds or wrap before the cost calculation.

This change:

- performs intermediate cost arithmetic using "u128";
- safely converts the final rounded cent values back to "u64";
- preserves the existing rounding behavior for normal values;
- adds regression coverage at the previous overflow threshold, immediately above it, and at "u64::MAX".

Addresses the Kinovi cost-overflow finding in #2271.

Replace the eager GIF/WebP "collect_frames()" path with incremental sampling so preview limits apply while frames are decoded rather than after every full-size frame has already been retained.

This change:

- bounds animated-preview response bodies to 64 MiB, including chunked responses;
- rejects animation canvases/frames above a 16 MP source-frame budget;
- caps cumulative decoded RGBA data at 512 MiB;
- retains at most 48 resized preview frames using progressive sampling while preserving skipped-frame timing;
- distinguishes non-animated input from budget/decode rejection so rejected animations do not fall back into the generic still-image decoder.

Regression coverage includes the streaming frame cap and duration preservation, cumulative decoded-byte cutoff, oversized-frame rejection, single-frame fallback behavior, and an encoded GIF through the real decoder path.

Validation note: "cargo test" could not be executed because the available remote environment does not include a Rust toolchain. The touched APIs were checked against the repository's "reqwest 0.12.23" and "image 0.25" interfaces.

Closes #2253

Honor the configured request-mismatch strategy when building Flux 2 LoRA angle requests.

Previously, "vertical_angle" was always clamped to the supported "0..=60" range. This meant requests using "ErrorOut" silently changed invalid values instead of reporting the unsupported option.

This change:

- rejects out-of-range vertical angles when using "ErrorOut";
- preserves clamping for permissive mitigation strategies;
- preserves valid vertical angles unchanged;
- adds regression tests for values below and above the supported range;
- updates the shared test fixture to use a valid strict-mode angle.

Addresses the strict image-angle contract finding in #2271.

Preserve the caller's explicit audio preference when building ArtCraft Seedance 2.0 requests.

The shared ArtCraft video request builder initializes "generate_audio" to "None". Unlike other audio-capable model builders, Seedance 2.0 did not restore the value from "GenerateVideoRequestBuilder", so explicit "true" or "false" selections were silently replaced by the server default.

This change:

- captures the Seedance 2.0 "generate_audio" preference before invoking the shared builder;
- restores it on the final request;
- preserves "None" when the caller leaves the preference unspecified;
- adds regression coverage for "None", "false", and "true".

Addresses the Seedance 2.0 audio-preference finding in #2271.

Recover Midjourney completion state before applying the one-hour job expiry policy.

Previously, jobs older than the expiry threshold were failed and removed before the Midjourney feed was queried. Because the finished-job set is memory-only, a completed job restored after an application restart could therefore be abandoned before its provider completion was rediscovered.

This change:

- includes overdue jobs in completion recovery;
- forces a Midjourney feed check before considering an overdue unseen job expired;
- only applies expiry after that recovery feed check succeeds;
- keeps jobs pending when the recovery request itself fails;
- preserves recovered completed jobs so their remaining result uploads can resume;
- adds focused regression coverage for recovery and expiry decisions.

Addresses the Midjourney recovery/expiry finding in #2266.

Terminate FAL provider jobs when polling returns an error that cannot recover on a later retry.

Previously, status and result polling treated every FAL error as transient. Permanent request failures could therefore leave a job stored indefinitely and trigger another polling request every worker cycle.

This change:

- treats malformed or invalid persisted polling URLs as terminal;
- treats job-specific HTTP `400`, `404`, `410`, and `422` responses as terminal;
- removes terminal jobs and reports the failure through the existing provider-job failure path;
- keeps authentication errors, rate limiting, request timeouts, server errors, transport failures, and other uncertain conditions retryable;
- preserves FAL's explicit `IncompleteJob` / “still in progress” response as retryable;
- adds regression coverage for terminal versus recoverable classifications.

Addresses the FAL permanent-error finding in #2266.

Preserve the caller-provided MIME type when uploading images through the Sora session auto-renew path.

The auto-renew request already carries an explicit `mime_type`, but both the initial upload and credential-renewed retry previously discarded it and inferred the type from the filename instead. Extensionless files and unsupported filename extensions could therefore be sent as `application/octet-stream` even when their actual MIME type was known.

This change:

- adds a backward-compatible upload helper that accepts an explicit MIME type;
- keeps the existing filename-inference helper unchanged for legacy callers;
- passes the request MIME type through the initial auto-renew upload;
- preserves the same MIME type when retrying with refreshed credentials;
- adds regression coverage showing explicit MIME values override filename inference while legacy fallback behavior remains intact.

Addresses the Sora upload MIME-type finding in #2271.

Move the desktop application's Cargo build profiles from its workspace-member manifest to the workspace root.

Cargo ignores `[profile.*]` sections defined inside workspace member manifests. As a result, the existing development and release settings for the desktop application were not being applied.

This change:

- moves the existing `[profile.dev]` configuration to the root `Cargo.toml`;
- moves the existing `[profile.release]` configuration to the root `Cargo.toml`;
- preserves the existing profile values unchanged;
- removes the ignored profile definitions from `crates/desktop/artcraft/Cargo.toml`.

This allows the intended incremental development and release optimization settings to actually participate in workspace builds.

Addresses the workspace release-profile finding in #2271.
